tips-dataset-1 = If you're on fire, you can click the alert on the right side of the screen to stop, drop, and roll.
tips-dataset-2 = You can view and edit keybinds at any time from the Options menu.
tips-dataset-3 = The Guidebook contains useful information on a variety of rules and gameplay mechanics, and can be accessed from the game menu or with the Open Guidebook keybind.
tips-dataset-4 = Some entities have Guidebook entries associated with them, which you can view by examining the entity and clicking the help icon.
tips-dataset-5 = Artifacts can sometimes permanently gain new properties when a node is activated. These properties might be useful, dangerous, annoying, and/or entertaining.
tips-dataset-6 = You can avoid slipping on most puddles by walking. However, puddles of especially slippery reagents (such as a space lube) will cause you to slip regardless.
tips-dataset-7 = Some plants, such as aloe and galaxythistle, can be processed into potent medicines.
tips-dataset-8 = After mopping a puddle, you can drain the mop into another container to recover the reagents the puddle contained.
tips-dataset-9 = Floor drains, such as those in the chef's freezer or janitor's office, automatically absorb puddles around them — including blood.
tips-dataset-10 = Cognizine, a hard-to-manufacture medicine, can be administered to animals to grant them sapience and the ability to speak!
tips-dataset-11 = Mousetraps are effective at dealing with all manner of tiny critters — including rat servants.
tips-dataset-12 = Fire extinguishers can be refilled with any arbitrary reagent — though some reagents will make a fire even worse!
tips-dataset-13 = Some reagents, such as chlorine trifluoride, react on touch, including when applied via a spray bottle or a cloud of foam.
tips-dataset-14 = Remember to take a break from Space Station 14 every once in a while.
tips-dataset-15 = You can use the Activate in World keybind to interact with objects while your hands are full, or without picking them up.
tips-dataset-16 = Common sense goes a long way to avoiding conflict.
tips-dataset-17 = Remember that every other character in the game is played by a real human being. Don't treat other people like NPCs!
tips-dataset-18 = Going for too long without eating or drinking anything will make you move slower. Remember to visit the bar and kitchen every now and then!
tips-dataset-19 = If you plan to harvest power from the singularity, remember to refuel the nearby radiation collectors with plasma gas every now and then. Gas tank dispensers in Engineering usually contain extra plasma tanks, and empty tanks can be refilled from plasma canisters.
tips-dataset-20 = Chemicals don't react with each other while inside the ChemMaster's buffer.
tips-dataset-21 = Don't anger the bartender by throwing their glasses! Politely place them on the table with the Drop Item keybind.
tips-dataset-22 = You can use the Brake keybind when piloting a shuttle to assist with small, precise movements, or to just make a complete stop.
tips-dataset-23 = Dexalin plus and epinephrine will purge heartbreaker toxin from the bloodstream.
tips-dataset-24 = Every crew member's survival box contains a single-use emergency medipen that can stop bleeding and help stabilize critical injuries.
tips-dataset-25 = The AME can produce a lot of power, but uses fuel quickly and can explode if overfueled. It's best used alongside other power sources, or as a temporary stopgap when no other power sources are functional.
tips-dataset-26 = If the tesla escapes containment, it will be drawn towards any nearby creatures and machines. Turning off your electronics does not make you less of a target; your safest bet is to be as far away as possible.
tips-dataset-27 = You can use a hand labeler to add labels to any item, including reagent jugs and pill canisters.
tips-dataset-28 = Riot armor provides excellent protection against blunt impacts and bladed weapons, but isn't especially bulletproof.
tips-dataset-29 = As a ghost, you can use the verb menu to orbit around and follow other entities.
tips-dataset-30 = As a Syndicate Agent, you may sometimes be given orders to hunt other agents. Likewise, other agents might be given orders to hunt you!
tips-dataset-31 = As a Syndicate Agent, a blood-red encryption key can be installed in a headset to grant access to the syndicate radio frequency, allowing for secure long-distance communications.
tips-dataset-32 = As an antagonist, consider compromising important radio frequencies to listen in on the station's plans, or sabotage them to make it difficult for the crew to coordinate.
tips-dataset-33 = As a Syndicate Agent, the suspicious toolbox provides a full set of tools, insulated combat gloves, and a syndicate gas mask. It's a versatile and low-cost way to bypass obstacles such as walls and airlocks.
tips-dataset-34 = As a Syndicate Agent, never underestimate the web vest. It may not protect from the vacuum of space, but its low cost and robust protection makes it handy when dealing with trigger-happy foes.
tips-dataset-35 = As a Syndicate Agent, grenade penguins will explode if killed, and will violently attack anyone nearby other than you.
tips-dataset-36 = As a Syndicate Agent, the chemical synthesis kit contains vestine, which can be refined into a variety of potent drugs and toxins. Vestine and its refined forms are known Syndicate contraband, so keep them well-hidden!
tips-dataset-37 = As a Syndicate Agent, remember that power sinks will create a loud sound and alert the crew after running for long enough. Try to hide them in a tricky-to-find spot, or reinforce the area around them so that they're harder to reach.
tips-dataset-38 = As an antagonist, remember that plasma gas is toxic when inhaled and extremely flammable. Flooding a room with plasma gas is an excellent way to sow chaos.
tips-dataset-39 = As a Syndicate Agent, a swarm of dehydrated carps can be an excellent distraction. Remember to pat them on the head before rehydrating them, or else they'll attack you too!
tips-dataset-40 = As an antagonist, injecting plasma into a power cell will cause it to explode when charged or discharged. A defibrilator sabotaged like this will explode on use, hurting both the user and the patient!
tips-dataset-41 = As a Nuclear Operative, stick together! Your fellow operatives can watch your back, provide covering fire, and drag you away from danger if you become incapacitated.
tips-dataset-42 = As a Nuclear Operative, communication is key! Use your radio to speak with your fellow operatives and coordinate an attack plan.
tips-dataset-43 = As a Nuclear Operative, remember that stealth is an option. It'll be hard for the captain to fight back if they get caught by surprise!
tips-dataset-44 = As a Nuclear Operative, remember that the station's crew heavily outnumber you. While your equipment is extremely powerful, being overly aggressive without a good plan can result in you being surrounded and overwhelmed.
tips-dataset-45 = You can examine a headset to see which radio frequencies it has encryption keys for, and how to speak on them.
tips-dataset-46 = As a Salvage Specialist, remember that the AstroNav app on your PDA allows it to function as a GPS. Take note of the station's coordinates to make it easier to find your way back if you get lost in space.
tips-dataset-47 = As a Cargo Technician, remember to check what you have in storage and what the autolathe can print before approving orders. If Engineering places an order for steel, and you have a big stockpile of steel sitting around unused, it's cheaper and faster to just give them some of that.
tips-dataset-48 = As a Salvage Specialist, remember to mine ore! Ore can be refined into usable materials by the ore processor. Some materials, such as uranium and bananium, can only be obtained by mining.
tips-dataset-49 = As a Salvage Specialist, remember that you can use a grappling hook and magboots to pull grids closer to the station, making them easier to access.
tips-dataset-50 = Grappling hooks and jetpacks can be used to easily navigate zero-gravity environments.
tips-dataset-51 = Tip #51 does not exist and has never existed. Ignore any rumors to the contrary.
tips-dataset-52 = As a Salvage Specialist, remember to work alongside the rest of the station! The salvage you bring back can be sold for money or recycled into raw materials, allowing the station to purchase or craft powerful equipment.
tips-dataset-53 = As a Cargo Technician, the Ripley APLU can use an installed hydraulic clamp to carry multiple crates (or similarly large objects) simultaneously.
tips-dataset-54 = As a Cargo Technician, remember to distribute surplus materials instead of just hoarding them. Other departments will almost always appreciate an unannounced delivery!
tips-dataset-55 = If your department has a massive budget surplus, try playing the grand lottery! You can find all sorts of interesting things inside, such as fashionable clothing, rare toys, illegal contraband, live animals, or even the Throngler.
tips-dataset-56 = Remember that request computers can be used to order supplies in an emergency! Purchasing weapons, armor, pressure suits, medical supplies, and/or other valuable equipment can often be the difference between you and your fellow crewmates living or dying.
tips-dataset-57 = As the Bartender, you can use a saw to saw off your shotgun's barrels, making it more compact but less accurate.
tips-dataset-58 = As the Bartender, try experimenting with unique drinks. Have you tried to make demon's blood yet?
tips-dataset-59 = As a Botanist, you can mutate and crossbreed plants together to create more potent produce that also has higher yields.
tips-dataset-60 = As the Clown, switch your gimmicks up! Nobody likes a one-trick pony.
tips-dataset-61 = As the Clown, if you don't have any banana peels or soap, you can still slip people with your PDA! Honk!
tips-dataset-62 = As the Chef, your cleaver is a surprisingly deadly weapon. Use it to deal with dangerous animals, or to chase unwanted visitors out of your kitchen.
tips-dataset-63 = Any arbitrary reagent can be poured onto most foods. This can be used to season meals to your liking — or to poison them.
tips-dataset-64 = As the Mime, your vow of silence grants you the ability to manifest literal invisible walls. Breaking your vow robs you of both your powers and your honor.
tips-dataset-65 = As the Lawyer, try to negotiate with Security if a criminal's punishment seems too severe for the crimes they (allegedly) committed.
tips-dataset-66 = As a Security Officer, communicate and coordinate with your fellow officers using the security radio frequency to avoid confusion.
tips-dataset-67 = As a Security Officer, remember that correlation does not equal causation. Someone may have just been at the wrong place at the wrong time!
tips-dataset-68 = As a Detective, you can identify criminals more effectively by looking at forensic scans of objects they likely interacted with. Fingerprints and DNA evidence are especially conclusive.
tips-dataset-69 = As an Atmospheric Technician, your ATMOS holofan projector blocks gases while allowing objects to pass through. With it, you can quickly contain gas spills, fires, and hull breaches.
tips-dataset-70 = As an Atmospheric Technician, try to resist the temptation of making canister bombs for self-defense, unless you're in a last-ditch scenario. They often lead to large amounts of unnecessary friendly fire!
tips-dataset-71 = You can use a welding tool to repair cracked windows, or to refine shards of broken glass into usable glass sheets.
tips-dataset-72 = Grilles with power cables running beneath them will become electrified, shocking anything that comes into contact with them.
tips-dataset-73 = As a Station Engineer, remember that the singularity and tesla produce a lot of power, but require containment fields to operate safely. Make sure to check on them periodically, or they could destroy the entire station!
tips-dataset-74 = Windows made from plasma glass or uranium glass are stronger than normal and will shield against radiation.
tips-dataset-75 = As the Captain, you are one of the highest-priority targets on the station. Antagonists of all kinds may try to rob you of your unique equipment and all-access ID card, or even outright assassinate you.
tips-dataset-76 = As the Captain, the nuclear authentication disk can be used by hostile actors to destroy the entire station, and its exact location can be tracked by pinpointers. Make sure the disk is always in your possession, or the possession of someone you can trust to keep it safe.
tips-dataset-77 = As the Captain, you have full authority over the entire station. However, you are not above Space Law, and the crew may elect to stage a mutiny if you abuse your power.
tips-dataset-78 = As the Captain, try to be active and patrol the station. Staying in one place makes it easier for would-be thieves and assassins to find you.
tips-dataset-79 = As a Scientist, you can use the node scanner to see when an artifact is able to react to triggers, and which triggers it is currently reacting to; each number it shows corresponds to a different node.
tips-dataset-80 = As a Scientist, you can research and construct upgraded machines to benefit the station. Salvage will love you if you upgrade their ore processor!
tips-dataset-81 = As a Scientist, you can construct cyborgs to aid in specialized tasks.
tips-dataset-82 = As a Medical Doctor, try to be wary of overdosing your patients, especially if someone else has already been on the scene. Overdoses are often lethal to patients in crit!
tips-dataset-83 = As a Medical Doctor, don't underestimate the cryo pods! Proper cryogenic treatment can quickly and efficiently treat many types of damage, and can even heal dead and rotting corpses with the proper medication.
tips-dataset-84 = As a Medical Doctor, remember that reptilians are more sensitive to cold temperatures, including when treated in a cryo pod. You can mitigate this with medication such as leporazine.
tips-dataset-85 = As a Medical Doctor, remember that the health analyzer can be used if you lose your PDA.
tips-dataset-86 = As a Chemist, once you've made everything you've needed to, don't be afraid to make more silly reagents. Have you tried desoxyephedrine or licoxide?
tips-dataset-87 = As a Medical Doctor, Chemist, or Chief Medical Officer, you can use chloral hydrate to nonlethally sedate unruly patients.
tips-dataset-88 = Don't be afraid to ask for help! You can ask other players for advice, either in-character or through LOOC chat, and you can use the Open Admin Help keybind to directly message game admins.
tips-dataset-89 = You'll quickly lose your interest in the game if you play to win and kill. If you find yourself doing this, take a step back and talk to people — it's a much better experience!
tips-dataset-90 = If there's something you need from another department, try asking. You'd be surprised what you can get accomplished together!
tips-dataset-91 = The station's nuke is invincible. If armed, the only way to stop it from exploding is to defuse it by hand.
tips-dataset-92 = Maintenance is full of equipment that is randomized every round. Look around and see if anything is worth using.
tips-dataset-93 = We were all new once. Be patient and guide new players, especially those playing trainee roles, in the right direction.
tips-dataset-94 = Fire suits, winter coats, and emergency pressure suits offer mild protection from cold temperatures.
tips-dataset-95 = Emergency closets and fire-safety closets will always contain emergency pressure suits and fire suits, respectively. They might be awkward to move around in, but can easily save your life in a dangerous situation.
tips-dataset-96 = In an emergency, remember that you can craft improvised weapons! A baseball bat or spear could easily mean the difference between deterring an attacker or perishing to one.
tips-dataset-97 = Spears can be tipped with chemicals, and will inject a few units every time you hit someone with them.
tips-dataset-98 = You can make spears with reinforced glass, plasma glass, or uranium glass shards to enhance their damage.
tips-dataset-99 = Thrown spears deal extra damage! Beware, however, as they'll break after a few throws.
tips-dataset-100 = Compared to other damage types, caustic, cellular, and radiation damage are hard to resist and slow and inconvenient to treat. This can be used to your advantage in combat.
tips-dataset-101 = You can throw bolas at people to slow them down. This makes it easier to chase someone, or to outrun a threat that's chasing you.
tips-dataset-102 = You can put napalm in a backpack water tank to make an improvised flamethrower.
tips-dataset-103 = Most jobs have alternative uniforms available in the Loadout menu, or in their associated wardrobe vendors. Don't be afraid to try out a new look!
tips-dataset-104 = Movement speed is extremely important in combat. Wearing a hardsuit in a fight is usually detrimental unless it's heavily armored, or you're not planning to go head-first into the fray.
tips-dataset-105 = Just because a job can't be an antagonist at the beginning of a round doesn't mean that they can't be converted into one, or that an antagonist can't impersonate a member of that job.
tips-dataset-106 = Syndicate gas masks will both provide welding protection and block flashes. Think twice before trying to flash a Nuclear Operative!
tips-dataset-107 = Demoman takes skill.
tips-dataset-108 = You can use the recoil from spraying a fire extinguisher, throwing items, or shooting a gun to move around in zero-gravity environments. Simply aim opposite the direction you want to go.
tips-dataset-109 = You can drag other players onto yourself to open the strip menu, allowing you to remove their equipment or force them to wear something. Note that exosuits and their helmets will block your access to the clothing beneath them, and that certain items take longer to strip or put on than others.
tips-dataset-110 = You can vault over a table or railing by dragging and dropping your character's sprite onto it, or with the Alternate Activate Item In World keybind.
tips-dataset-111 = You can use the Moved Pulled Object keybind to move an object you're pulling towards your mouse. This is useful for precisely positioning an object, or dragging an obstacle out of your path.
tips-dataset-112 = When dealing with security, you can often get your sentence negated entirely through cooperation and deception.
tips-dataset-113 = Fire spreads! Be careful not to get too close to people or animals that are on fire.
tips-dataset-114 = Hull breaches take a few seconds to fully space an area. You can use this time to flee, or to attempt to fix the hole.
tips-dataset-115 = Burn damage, such as from a welding tool or lightbulb, can be used to cauterize wounds and stop bleeding.
tips-dataset-116 = Bleeding is no joke! If you're heavily bleeding, patch yourself up or seek medical treatment as soon as possible.
tips-dataset-117 = In an emergency, you can butcher clothing with a sharp object to get cloth, which can be crafted into gauze.
tips-dataset-118 = You can use a sharp object, such as a knife or a shard of glass, to cut up clothing or butcher animals.
tips-dataset-119 = Most explosives have an adjustable timer that you can set in the right click menu. This includes grenade penguins!
tips-dataset-120 = You can stun grenade penguins, which can bide valuable time to retreat to a safe distance.
tips-dataset-121 = Interacting with an item's name in the verb menu is treated the same as interacting with the item itself in the world. For instance, you can click on an item's name to pick up.
tips-dataset-122 = Space Station 14 is open source! If there's a change you want to make, or a simple item you want to add, then try contributing to the game. It's not as hard as you'd think it is.
tips-dataset-123 = In a pinch, you can throw drinks or other reagent containers behind you to create a spill that can slip people chasing you.
tips-dataset-124 = Lightweight melee weapons, such as knives, are easy to carry and can be swung rapidly, but tend not to do as much damage per hit.
tips-dataset-125 = Firelocks and unpowered airlocks can be pried open with a prying tool, such as a crowbar. Jaws of life can additionally pry open powered airlocks, making them extremely useful for forcing your way into restricted areas.
tips-dataset-126 = If you're not a human, you can drink blood to heal back some of your blood volume, albeit very inefficiently.
tips-dataset-127 = If you're a human, don't drink blood! It makes you sick and you'll begin to take damage.
tips-dataset-128 = There is a chemical metabolism limit that limits the amount of reagents of a certain type your body can process. Some species have higher metabolism limits than others.
tips-dataset-129 = Welding without proper eye protection can cause eye damage, which must be cured with oculine.
tips-dataset-130 = Zombies are very vulnerable to heat damage, making welding tools and laser weapons extremely effective against them.
tips-dataset-131 = Zombies are too clumsy to use their hands for most tasks, but can pry open airlocks bare-handed, even if they're powered.
tips-dataset-132 = By examining a player (including yourself) and clicking the health icon, you can get a visual description of how badly they're hurt. Note that some damage types are more visually obvious than others.
tips-dataset-133 = Monkeys and kobolds have a rare chance to be sapient.
tips-dataset-134 = The lights on firelocks will indicate if there's a dangerous difference in air pressure or temperature between each side.
tips-dataset-135 = You can use the Alternate Activate Item In World keybind to eat food without picking it up. This is especially useful for creatures without hands, such as mice.
tips-dataset-136 = If you're trapped behind an electrified airlock, you can throw your ID at it to open it from a distance, or shut off the power to safely pry it open.
tips-dataset-137 = If the AI electrifies an airlock and you have insulated gloves, you can snip and mend the power wire to reset the door's electrification.
tips-dataset-138 = Turning on combat mode while uncuffing someone will shove them down as soon as the uncuffing is complete, making it difficult for them to immediately attempt to escape or attack you.
tips-dataset-139 = Make sure to clean your illegal implanters with a soap after you use them! Detectives can scan used implanters for incriminating DNA evidence, but not if they've been wiped clean.
tips-dataset-140 = Two-handed melee weapons, such as fire axes, hit very hard. However, they can't be swung very rapidly and are typically awkward to carry around.
tips-dataset-141 = As a Changeling, you can transform even while moving. Be careful not to let anyone see you!
tips-dataset-142 = As a Changeling, remember that you're not protected by Space Law! Revealing your true nature is likely to result in the station hunting you down.
tips-dataset-143 = Changelings cannot be gibbed, but can still be ashed by high temperatures.
tips-dataset-144 = As a Changeling, try to sabotage the crematorium so that the crew has a harder time ashing you!
tips-dataset-145 = A Changeling's armblade can be used as a prying tool, and can even pry open powered airlocks.
tips-dataset-146 = Be careful not to let Changelings roam free on the station. They can evolve their Horror Form, making them nigh-invulnerable and giving them a powerful screech ability for a limited duration.
tips-dataset-147 = Changelings have a limit on the number of identities they can store at once; if they want to devour more, they will have to forget one of them.
tips-dataset-148 = Changelings have an ability to store bodies inside of a fleshy mass. This preserves the body of the victim, but also passively generates them DNA points. Make sure to collect them often!
tips-dataset-149 = Changeling headslugs are known to infect some meteors!
tips-dataset-150 = Changelings are only truly dead once their body is completely destroyed.
tips-dataset-151 = Changelings have evolved to evade simple centrifuge tests. Centrifuging Changeling blood does nothing. Stop asking. Seriously.
tips-dataset-152 = Changelings can see in the dark. Be wary of what lurks in the maintenance shafts...
tips-dataset-153 = The Head of Security is a Changeling! You're a Changeling too!
tips-dataset-154 = Changelings can stealthily "sting" victims with a variety of chemicals, with effects ranging from slow death to the growth of an armblade!
tips-dataset-155 = When devouring someone, Changelings spread living tissue over the floor, which can be chemically processed into hivarol. When injected with hivarol, Changelings will uncontrollably scream and shake, even if disguised!
tips-dataset-156 = A thing from space! Changelings can shapeshift to perfectly mimic the physical appearance of their victims. Be wary of your coworkers.
tips-dataset-157 = The "tip" console command can be used at any time to instantly display a random tip.
